what is the key interest or lending rate  it influences how cheap loans are how  much money is in circulation and whether  the economy grows or shrinks the  benchmark lending rate is the most  important interest rate for the money  markets it's set by the central banks in  Europe  that's the ECB in the u.s. it's the Fed  if the benchmark lending rate is low  banks can borrow money cheaply from the  central bank and then lend it to  businesses and private individuals and  more and more people are taking out  loans to buy a car build a house invest  in the stock market or purchase other  consumer goods that leads to a growth in  industry and creates jobs but when  there's lots of money in circulation the  risk of inflation grows to keep  inflation in check the central bank  needs to raise its benchmark lending  rate but that makes loans more expensive  and slows down the economy heavily  indebted countries can find themselves  struggling to carry their huge interest  burden but in principle at least an  independent central bank shouldn't  really be swayed by such concerns its  job is to keep prices stable  that's why it must either lower or raise  its benchmark lending rate to regulate  the financial markets
 

